 "meta_notes": {
  "scope": "doc 32 SS5: NOT quantum advantage. One rung, one task family, one dataset, simulation only, 6 verbs, 5 seeds. B5 is dimension-matched and unconstrained -- a stronger comparator than B1 ever was -- so a positive licenses only 'the two-qubit register class generalises on this rung where the dimension-matched classical linear class does not, at matched referent dimension and with the comparator given more parameters and more search'. A4's L2 gate pass was by EXACTLY ZERO margin on train and this doc does not repair that: if tier-m passes, the underlying learnability remains knife-edge and both facts travel together. B5 is not 'the classical method' -- sequence models and anything with a different inductive bias are untested here. No hardware reading follows.",
  "search_asymmetry": "doc 32 SS2, DELIBERATE and in the COMPARATOR's favour: A4 runs at ONE frozen config while B5 gets the full 4-config grid with per-seed val-only selection. B5's four configs sat within 0.005 of each other on Phase-1 mean best-val, so fixing one would be near-arbitrary; giving it all four can only raise its score. Must be stated in every report (Rule 5).",
  "content_rule": "doc 32 SS4, frozen: if content_earned_A4 is FALSE, A4 solves L2 without using verb identity. That is the MORE IMPORTANT finding of the two, it leads the write-up, and it bears retroactively on how every A4 result is described."
